## Tasks Are the Source of Truth

<!--
  guidance: This is the single most load-bearing idea in the whole file. Chat history
  is not durable — it gets summarized, truncated, or lost across context resets and
  across sessions. If "what's done, what's next, and who's doing it" only lives in
  the conversation, every new session (or every agent you spawn) starts from a guess.

  A task list (a TODO tool, an issue tracker, a tasks.json, whatever your tooling
  provides) is durable and inspectable independent of any one conversation. Treat it
  as the ONE place that answers "what is the current state of this project" — richer
  than a memory summary, and it doesn't drift the way prose notes do.
-->

- Before starting any work, check the current task list. Don't start from what the
  conversation *implies* is next — start from what the task list *says* is next.
- Before modifying files, mark the task you're working on as in-progress. This is
  what lets a second agent (or a second session of you) see that it's claimed.
- After finishing, mark it done — including a short note on what actually changed,
  not just "done." Future-you and other agents rely on that note more than the diff.
- If work doesn't map to an existing task, create one before you start. Undocumented
  work is invisible work — it didn't happen, as far as the next session is concerned.

