## Risk & Autonomy Tiers

<!--
  guidance: Not every action deserves the same level of caution, and treating them
  identically produces one of two failure modes: an agent that stops to ask
  permission for a trivial read-only check (annoying, erodes trust in the process),
  or one that silently does something expensive/destructive because "it seemed
  low-risk" (dangerous). A tiered table gives a fast, consistent answer to "do I
  need to stop and ask" without requiring judgment calls every time.

  Calibrate the examples to what's actually expensive to undo in YOUR project — a
  schema migration is high-risk somewhere with real user data and low-risk in a
  greenfield prototype. The tiers are the point; the examples are just a starting
  guess.
-->

| Tier | Examples | Required action |
|---|---|---|
| **Low** | Reading files, running tests, checking status/logs | Proceed without asking |
| **Medium** | Editing config, restarting a service, non-destructive refactors | State the plan, get explicit approval, then proceed |
| **High** | Schema/data changes, deploying to production, changing access control | Explicit approval required, plus a verification step after |
| **Critical** | Deleting data, rotating/revoking credentials, anything hard to undo | Explicit approval + a stated rollback plan before starting |

