## Agent Routing

<!--
  guidance: This section only applies if you're running more than one specialized
  agent (a multi-agent / sub-agent setup) rather than a single general-purpose
  Claude Code session. If you're not doing that yet, delete this section — add it
  back when you are.

  The problem it solves: as an agent fleet grows, "which agent handles this kind of
  task" stops being obvious, and without a routing table, work either goes to a
  general-purpose agent that does a mediocre job at something specialized, or
  everyone just guesses. A routing table makes the assignment mechanical and
  auditable instead of a fresh judgment call every time.
-->

| Task type | Agent | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| {{TASK_TYPE_1}} (e.g. backend API work) | `{{AGENT_1}}` | {{WHEN_TO_USE}} |
| {{TASK_TYPE_2}} (e.g. security review) | `{{AGENT_2}}` | {{WHEN_TO_USE}} |
| {{TASK_TYPE_3}} (e.g. test coverage) | `{{AGENT_3}}` | {{WHEN_TO_USE}} |
| Anything that doesn't clearly fit above | `{{GENERAL_PURPOSE_AGENT}}` | Default fallback — don't force a specialist fit that isn't there |

Assign the agent when the task is created, not after work has started. If a task
turns out to need a different specialist than assigned, reassign it explicitly and
say why — don't just have the wrong agent push through anyway.

