## Hook Enforcement

<!--
  guidance: A rule that only lives in this file is a rule an agent can miss, forget
  mid-task, or (for a less careful model) rationalize around under time pressure.
  Hooks are how you make a rule mechanical instead of aspirational: a pre-tool-use
  hook can block a write outside an in-progress task; a pre-commit hook can block a
  commit containing a secret pattern regardless of what any agent "meant" to do.

  Not everything needs to be a hook — plenty of rules are fine as prose guidance
  that a careful agent follows. Reserve hooks for the rules where a MISS is
  expensive: secrets leaking, destructive commands running unreviewed, writes
  happening with no task backing them. If you don't have hook infrastructure yet,
  keep this section as a statement of intent and revisit once you do.
-->

This project enforces the rules above with hooks, not just documentation, where the
cost of a miss is high:

| Hook | Enforces |
|---|---|
| {{HOOK_1_NAME}} | {{WHAT_IT_BLOCKS_OR_CHECKS}} |
| {{HOOK_2_NAME}} | {{WHAT_IT_BLOCKS_OR_CHECKS}} |

Hooks are the backstop, not the first line of defense — read this file and follow it
directly; don't rely on a hook to catch what good judgment should have caught first.

