## Secrets Discipline

<!--
  guidance: State this explicitly rather than assuming it's obvious. "Never hardcode
  secrets" is easy to agree with in the abstract and easy to violate in a rush —
  pasting a token into a one-off script "just to test something," or logging a full
  request object that happens to contain an Authorization header. Naming the actual
  mechanism (where secrets live, how they're loaded) turns the rule from a vague
  value into a specific, checkable habit.
-->

- Secrets live in `{{SECRETS_LOCATION}}` (e.g. an untracked `.env`, a secrets
  manager, a vault) — never in source, never in commit history, never in a config
  file that gets checked in.
- Load them via `{{SECRETS_LOAD_COMMAND}}` before running anything that needs them.
- If a secret ever lands in a diff, a log, or a commit — even briefly, even in a
  branch that gets reverted — treat it as compromised and rotate it. "I removed it
  in the next commit" does not undo the exposure.

